cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its reported results, please cite this repository and record the commit used."
type: software
title: "Counterexample-guided operator coalescing: a controlled study of LLM weight sharing"
authors:
  - family-names: Legotin
    given-names: Artem
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-10-28"
repository-code: "https://github.com/arlegotin/counterexample-guided-operator-coalescing"
url: "https://github.com/arlegotin/counterexample-guided-operator-coalescing"
license:
  - AGPL-3.0-only
  - CC-BY-4.0
abstract: >-
  Research software for sharing nonlinear feed-forward operators across
  transformer layers with low-rank per-layer corrections. The local SmolLM2-135M
  study compares seven merge-scoring rules on the same twenty fitted candidate
  states, with fresh-document evaluation, source-component bootstrap selection
  uncertainty, exact fitted-state replay and native payload accounting.
  Ordinary natural KL, random-donor and two active-counterexample rules select
  the same pair within each fixed analysis; the selected pair changes on fresh
  documents. Active selection has no demonstrated advantage. The completed
  diagnostic is inconclusive because near-best selection uncertainty and fitting
  sensitivity remain unresolved. A separately bounded continuation repairs one
  merge over sixteen updates: ordinary-text fitting reduces validation KL from
  0.2232 to 0.1893, slightly outperforming the counterexample mixture. Matched
  native measurements favor dense 8-bit quantization over the new floating
  shared artifact. Trained shared quantizations fit the target byte ceiling but
  retain poor fidelity. No useful compression win, protected capability result
  or runtime advantage is established.
  Public receipts and nontext metric metadata support report-only regeneration
  without model weights or corpus access.
keywords:
  - language model compression
  - weight sharing
  - counterexamples
  - low-rank adaptation
  - reproducible research
  - inconclusive results
